{#-
  The prover's prompt template for the tool loop: the model
  works in a workspace using the tools its capabilities expose. One runtime
  file supplies every prompt, selected by `mode`:

    "system"  the system message of every run;
    "task"    the first user message of a run with no parent transcript;
    "resume"  the user message after the task, or appended to a parent's
              transcript when a continuation (CONTINUE(v)) starts from it.
    "nudge", "continue_after_length", "batch_hint", "seed_hint"
              ordinary no-tool, truncated-output and tool-result reminders.
    "context_reset"
              the notice after the conversation is cleared at the context
              threshold; there is no summary call.
    "collection_instruction", "collection_nudge", "collection_tool_description"
              the separate terminal collection phase (only with submit authority).
    "plan_publish", "plan_read", "plan_review"
              publishing and comparing live research plans (only with both
              plan tools).

  Runtime modes also receive `collection_calls` (the enforced phase bound) and
  `submit_tool` (the actual capability-listed tool name, or none). All required
  modes are rendered and validated at worker startup; no built-in text fallback.
  `publish_plan_tool` and `read_plans_tool` are the listed names, or none, in
  every mode; a board's contents are tool results, never template text.

  Everything else is the standard message transcript the worker keeps and
  the harness records (nothing is folded into the first message; a
  continuation appends).

  This file says what the model cannot know: the problem, this harness's
  rules, what is graded and what ends the run. It does not teach Lean, Mathlib
  or bash. Variables are `problem`, `statement`, `file`, `parent`,
  `inherited`, `check`, `siblings`, `siblings_total`, `calls_left`,
  `attempt`, `file_name`, the workspace file,
  `branch_references`, pointers to other completed branches, and
  `exec_tool` and `bash_timeout_ms`, the listed name of the command tool
  and the limit the harness gives a command. Strict
  rendering, nothing escaped, trim_blocks/lstrip_blocks on. The tools and
  the environment they run in are `services`: the kernel's listing of the
  tool-callable actions this worker holds, each service with its own
  description of itself — nothing about a tool is written here.
-#}
